    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/coricancha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/coricancha.jpg" alt="Coricancha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/coricancha">Coricancha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coricancha,_Cusco,_Per%C3%BA,_2015-07-31,_DD_68.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Inca religion was a group of beliefs and rites related to a mythological system that evolved from pre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> times into the Inca Empire, or Tawantinsuyu. Its followers were polytheists, with local, regional and pan-regional divinities, and faith showed itself in every part of life, work, festivals and ceremonies. The Incas took over and institutionalised the religious practices of older Andean societies, most notably the Wari, Chavín and Nazca cultures. The empire was preceded by two large-scale empires, the Tiwanaku (c. 300–1100 CE), based around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a>, and the Wari (c. 600–1100 CE), who occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a> area for about 400 years. Aspects of Andean religion reach beyond the empire's former borders to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuches</a> and Huilliches of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, which has raised the hypothesis of an earlier spread of Andean religion from Tiwanaku.</p><p>In the early 1200s, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-capac">Manco Capac</a>, the Inca formed the small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> Kingdom of Cuzco. There Manco Capac built a temple to the Sun God, called Inticancha, in the current location of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coricancha">Coricancha</a>. The Incas had a great number of origin stories, which historians and scholars find hard to sort out; they often contradict themselves. Many place the beginning of life at Lake Titicaca. One legend collected by the chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-garcilaso-de-la-vega">Inca Garcilaso de la Vega</a> has Manco Capac and Mama Ocllo sent by the Sun God, emerging from the lake.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>A theme of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> mythology is the duality of the cosmos. Hanan pacha, the upper world, held the deities of the sun, moon, stars, rainbow and lightning; ukhu pacha and urin pacha were the realms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachamama">Pachamama</a>, the earth mother, and of the ancestors and heroes of the Inca and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayllu">ayllus</a>; kay pacha, where humans lived, lay between them. The condor stood for the upper world, the puma for the outer earth and the snake for the inner earth. In Andean thought, asymmetrical dualism holds that reality is built by different forces that need each other to be complete, one slightly stronger than the other. Camac is the life force that inhabits everything, living or dead, in different quantities, and places and objects with special energy were called wak'a: mountains, boulders, streams, battlefields, pottery believed to carry a deity.</p><p>Among the official deities were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viracocha">Viracocha</a>, creator of humanity and everything else; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti">Inti</a>, the sun god, whose temple was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coricancha">Coricancha</a>, the Golden Enclosure; Illapa, who controlled weather, rain and lightning; Mamaquilla, Mother Moon and wife of Inti; Pachamama, the Earth Mother, protector of fields and crops; and Mama Cocha, Mother of Lakes. The Pleiades (Qollqa), mother of the stellar deities, marked the start of the agricultural year when they reappeared after 37 days unseen. Families sometimes kept household gods as small figures called chancas or conopas.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>No scriptures are recorded. Because of the Spanish conquest much direct evidence of Inca religion was lost, and modern historians rely heavily on colonial chronicles and on the archaeological remains of religious customs. The religion is therefore known largely through Spanish and colonial writers. The ritual duties of each shrine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> were recorded on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quipu">quipus</a>, knotted string recording devices. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-betanzos">Juan de Betanzos</a> is cited for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a>' turn to secrecy after the Spaniards arrived, and Garcilaso de la Vega for his sight of the embalmed kings at the house of Polo de Ondegardo.</p><p>One account of the origin has the creator god Wiraqocha Pachayachachic form giants at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a> to see whether humans would work well at that size. Finding that they did not, he made them of his own size. These humans were hubristic and greedy and were turned to stone or other forms, some engulfed by stone or sea. The Creator then summoned a great flood to destroy all life except three men, who would later help create humans again. At a later point at Lake Titicaca he made the sun, moon and stars. The moon shone brighter than the sun, and the sun, filled with jealousy, threw ashes in her face to dim her. The Creator then spread out with two servants to call forth the people of every nation, from every mountain, crevice, cave and lake, before walking over the water into the west.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pachacamac"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pachacamac.jpg" alt="Pachacamac" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pachacamac">Pachacamac</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pachacamac_-_51163366566.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apollo, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From 1438 to 1533 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> incorporated a large portion of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, by conquest and by peaceful assimilation. The expansion began under the ninth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti">Pachacuti</a>, who reorganised the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> into the Tahuantinsuyu. Religious traditions in the Andes varied among the different <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayllu">ayllus</a>. The Inca generally allowed, or even took in, the deities and heroes of the ayllus they conquered, but they also brought their own gods to those peoples by building them into law, for instance by requiring sacrifice, and tried to combine their deities with conquered ones in ways that raised the status of their own.</p><p>The Inca leadership encouraged the sun worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti">Inti</a> and imposed its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> above other religious groups, such as that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachamama">Pachamama</a>, who had been worshipped long before the rise of the Inca. The Incas considered their king, the Sapa Inca, to be the son of the Sun. Locals were allowed to worship their existing beings, through pilgrimages, offerings and other rites, while still providing the sacrifices and offerings due to the Sun god. Some local traditions, such as the Oracle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacamac">Pachacamac</a> on the coast, were officially venerated. The state religion was administrative and concentrated in Cusco, where a complex calendar set the state's festivals, holy days and major ceremonies.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huayna-capac.jpg" alt="Huayna Capac" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hp_inka11.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Felipe Huaman Puma de Ayala, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>'s sacred geography held 328 huacas, objects, landmarks or shrines in and around the valley, arranged along 41 or 42 radial pathways called ceques that all began at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coricancha">Coricancha</a>. Ten royal kindred groups of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> nobility, the panacas, served as the main priesthood in Cusco. They were responsible for the ancestral worship of dead emperors and the veneration of state deities, and were organised in two divisions of five, Hanan (Upper Cusco), north of the river, and Hurin (Lower Cusco), to the south. They took turns in ritual duties, offering sacrifices to the Sun, the Moon, Thunder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viracocha">Viracocha</a> and the Earth.</p><p>The Inca calendar had 12 months of 30 days, each with a festival, and a five-day feast at the end. The year began in December with Qhapaq Raymi, the first and biggest festival, when boys went through their puberty rites. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti-raymi">Inti Raymi</a>, at the June solstice, honoured the Sun god and ended with the ruler first to plough the earth.</p><p>Divination came before any action and nearly every rite was accompanied by sacrifices, usually <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> beer, food or llamas, but occasionally virgins or children. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">Human sacrifice</a>, usually of a child (qhapaq hucha) or a slave, is confirmed by archaeological remains on high mountain summits; specially chosen children were taken up to freezing peaks and left, either plied with alcohol or in some cases strangled. As many as 4,000 people were killed on the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a> in 1527.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui.jpg" alt="Túpac Inca Yupanqui" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui">Túpac Inca Yupanqui</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tupac-inca-yupanqui-small.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Felipe Huaman Puma de Ayala, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rulers were often mummified at death and worshipped within the palaces. Several times a year the mummies were seated in the order of their reigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a>'s plaza, and in other months they were returned to their palaces and visited. The ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> was expected to seek their advice on important matters. Not all mummies were honoured: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui">Topa Inca Yupanqui</a>'s was burned and his bloodline killed for siding with Huascar in the civil war.</p><p>The Spanish began the conquest in 1532, and the Incas began to hide the bodies of the kings and worship more secretly. Polo de Ondegardo, appointed Corregidor of Cuzco in 1559, tried to stop what he saw as the Incas' idolatrous sins, found most of the mummified kings and sent them to the viceroy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, where they stayed in a hospital for around 80 years before their whereabouts became unknown. Taki Unquy (c. 1564 to c. 1572), a movement that began in Huamanga, held that the wak'as would return and defeat the Spanish God. The visitador Cristóbal de Albornoz suppressed it harshly: its leader Juan Chocne and the other spiritual leaders were taken to Cusco to reject their beliefs in public, the women were imprisoned in convents and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuraka">kurakas</a> were fined. The last Inca stronghold fell in 1572. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachamama">Pachamama</a> is still described as a source of fertility and abundance in Bolivian indigenous belief.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Inca religion was the polytheist system of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>, growing out of older Andean traditions and officially centred on the cult of the Sun, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viracocha">Viracocha</a> as creator and sacred places called wak'a. After the Spanish conquest began in 1532 the royal mummies were hunted down and the Taki Unquy movement was suppressed.</em></p>

